 Unusual new agent prompts may initially show as `idle` instead of `blocked` until Herdr learns that screen shape. Detection itself does not send input, but automation that trusts a mistaken idle state can type into an approval dialog. Inspect unexpected prompts before sending more input; idle status is not authorization to approve an action.
 
-For OpenCode Mini, an active stop control in its status footer (`esc stop`, followed by the mode, model, and account/provider fields) counts as `working` only when that footer is the bottom non-empty row of the live buffer. The same text appearing earlier as quoted, prefixed, or otherwise embedded transcript content is not working evidence, and approval UI still wins over a visible busy footer. A bare line that is byte-identical to a real footer and sits at the bottom of the buffer cannot be told apart from the live footer. The rule recognizes the default block-spinner frames. An ASCII or mini.mono spinner frame does not match this footer rule; that footer alone is not treated as working, although other existing rules can still classify a screen as `working`. If no rule matches, the pane reads `idle`, which is the pre-existing behavior for that configuration. Because this is a screen-shape rule, not a request-completion protocol, it does not change `agent wait`'s lifecycle-state checks or fix the separate full-TUI premature-completion problem.
+For OpenCode Mini, an active stop control in its status footer (`esc stop`, followed by the mode and model fields) counts as `working` only when that footer is the bottom non-empty row of the live buffer. The footer may end in `· OpenCode`, an account/provider pair, or a usage token such as `· 6.7K (3%)`. The same text appearing earlier as quoted, prefixed, or otherwise embedded transcript content is not working evidence, and approval UI still wins over a visible busy footer. A bare line that is byte-identical to a real footer and sits at the bottom of the buffer cannot be told apart from the live footer. The rule recognizes the default block-spinner frames. An ASCII or mini.mono spinner frame does not match this footer rule; that footer alone is not treated as working, although other existing rules can still classify a screen as `working`. If no rule matches, the pane reads `idle`, which is the pre-existing behavior for that configuration. Because this is a screen-shape rule, not a request-completion protocol, it does not change `agent wait`'s lifecycle-state checks or fix the separate full-TUI premature-completion problem.
 
 Antigravity and Grok count as idle once they return to their prompt, even if background tasks, monitors, or subagents are still running. `agent wait` and `agent prompt --wait` observe the agent's state, not completion of those jobs.
 
